  "query_preview": "..Weak, diffuse low pressure is in place this evening\nacross AZ and extending into UT and eastern CO. This, combined\nwith PWATs near 0.7 inches allowed isolated to widely scattered\nweak convection to develop over mainly the southern Utah mountains\nthis afternoon which have now mostly dissipated, leaving some mid\nand high clouds in place that will continue over much of southern\nand eastern Utah into Friday morning.\n\nAn upper trough currently noted off the PacNW coast will make its\nway inland tonight, crossing ID Friday before ejecting across\nwestern MT. Ahead of this trough, isolated to widely scattered\nconvection can be expected once again Friday afternoon. These are\nexpected to develop over the southern and central Utah mountains,\nthen shift into the adjacent eastern valleys with the flow aloft \nturning more westerly. A shallow cold front will push into\nnorthwest Utah Friday night, bringing slightly cooler temperatures\nto that area through Saturday. \n\nWith generally dry and stable conditions expected on Saturday,\nattention then turns to the next trough, which will be deepening\noff the West Coast on Sunday. Ahead of this trough, a vort max\noriginating near Hurricane Odalys in the eastern Pacific is \nprogged to lift into Utah Sunday afternoon, bringing an increase \nin moisture and some widely scattered convection. The trough \nitself is progged to approach Utah by Monday. Latest deterministic\nEC/GFS and an increasing number of global ensemble members are \ntrending towards a splitting, closed-low evolution, which would \nbring the system farther south with a slower eastward progression.\nThis would result in the system being able to better tap moisture\nfrom Hurricanes Odalys and Polo, with longer duration \nprecipitation potentially aiding greater amounts. Widespread \nprecipitation appears likely across southern and eastern Utah, but\nat this time, quite a bit of uncertainty remains with regards to \nprecipitation amounts, with a large spread in the NBM 25th-75th \npercentile amounts. For example, many southeast Utah valley areas \nhave 48-hr totals Monday through Tuesday of 0.10 inches or less at\nthe 25th percentile but around 1 inch or more at the 75th \npercentile. However, even with the uncertainty, the amounts at the\nhigher end of the probabilistic spectrum have been climbing, \nmeaning increasingly greater amounts are becoming possible. \n\nBesides precipitation, the trough will bring cooler temperatures\ninto the area, with afternoon maxes falling 5-10 degrees below\nnormal for Monday and Tuesday, with many valleys reaching low 70s\nat best. Temperatures will slowly rebound midweek as the storm\nsystem exits the area, but significant spread exists in the large\nscale pattern for the second half of next week.",
